        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A new asphalt driveway does not need sealer in its first months, and coating it then tends to cause problems rather than prevent them. Let it get through a full warm season, protect the soft surface from point loads and scuffs, and watch for the gloss to fade, the surface to firm up, and water to start soaking in.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When those signs line up, seal it with a quality water-based product in thin coats on a dry, mild day. After that, a two-to-three-year sealing rhythm plus prompt crack repair each spring is what gets a residential driveway to 15 or 20 years and beyond.
          </p>
